### What is the delegation pattern?

Delegation is composition over inheritance applied as a concrete pattern.

### How does delegation differ from inheritance?

```java
// Inheritance: exposes everything from parent
public class Account {
    public void deposit(java.math.BigDecimal amount) { /* ... */ }
    public void withdraw(java.math.BigDecimal amount) { /* ... */ }
    public void transferInternational(String country, java.math.BigDecimal amount) { /* ... */ }
}
public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    // Inherits ALL: deposit, withdraw, transferInternational
    // transferInternational() is unwanted for savings but it is inherited and public.
}

// Delegation: forwards only what you choose
public class SavingsAccountService {
    private final Account delegate;

    public SavingsAccountService(Account delegate) { this.delegate = delegate; }

    public void deposit(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        if (amount.compareTo(new java.math.BigDecimal("10000000")) > 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Savings deposit limit: Rs.1 crore");
        }
        delegate.deposit(amount); // delegates the actual work
    }

    public void withdraw(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        delegate.withdraw(amount); // delegates
    }

    // transferInternational: deliberately NOT forwarded.
    // Caller cannot call savingsService.transferInternational().
}
```

### What are the three benefits?

```java
// Benefit 1: Control (expose only what makes sense)
// Inheritance: all parent methods visible. Cannot hide transferInternational.
// Delegation: forward deposit + withdraw. Hide everything else.

// Benefit 2: Enhancement (decorate before/after)
public void deposit(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
    log.info("Before deposit: {}", amount);
    validate(amount);
    delegate.deposit(amount); // delegate
    notifyCustomer("Deposited Rs." + amount);
}

// Benefit 3: Swappability (change delegate at runtime)
public class FlexibleService {
    private AccountOperations delegate;
    public void setDelegate(AccountOperations newDelegate) {
        this.delegate = newDelegate; // swap at runtime
    }
}
// Production: PostgresAccountOps. Testing: MockAccountOps.
```

### How does delegation compare to inheritance?

| Aspect | Inheritance | Delegation |
|--------|------------|------------|
| API exposure | All parent methods | Only forwarded methods |
| Flexibility | Fixed at compile time | Swappable at runtime |
| Enhancement | Override + super.method() (fragile) | Add logic around delegate call |
| Coupling | Tight (child depends on parent internals) | Loose (depends on interface) |

### What patterns use delegation?

- Decorator: wrapper implements same interface as delegate, adds behavior.
- Adapter: wrapper translates between incompatible interfaces.
- Proxy: wrapper controls access to the delegate (Spring @Transactional).

### What should a senior engineer know?

- In Spring, all @Transactional proxies use delegation: the proxy intercepts the call, starts a transaction, delegates to the real bean, and commits or rolls back.
- Kotlin has built-in delegation syntax: `class Stack<E>(private val list: MutableList<E>) : MutableList<E> by list`.
- Project Lombok's @Delegate annotation generates delegation boilerplate automatically.
